kSet</a>&gt;
            is used</li>
            <li>&lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#relatedWorksWrap">lido:relatedWorksWrap</a>&gt;
            if &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#relatedWorkSet">lido:relatedWorkSet</a>&gt;
            is used</li>
            <li>&lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#repositoryWrap">lido:repositoryWrap</a>&gt;
            if &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#repositorySet">lido:repositorySet</a>&gt;
            is used</li>
            <li>&lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#resourceWrap">lido:resourceWrap</a>&gt;
            if &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#resourceSet">lido:resourceSet</a>&gt;
            is used</li>
            <li>&lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#rightsWorkWrap">lido:rightsWorkWrap</a>&gt;
            if &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#rightsWorkSet">lido:rightsWorkSet</a>&gt;
            is used</li>
            <li>&lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#subjectWrap">lido:subjectWrap</a>&gt;
            if &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#subjectSet">lido:subjectSet</a>&gt;
            is used</li>
            <li>&lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#titleWrap">lido:titleWrap</a>&gt;
            if &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#titleSet">lido:titleSet</a>&gt;
            is used</li>
            </ul>
            <h3 data-number="5.1.2"
            id="lido-metadata-record-identifier-1"><span
            class="header-section-number">5.1.2</span> LIDO Metadata
            Record Identifier</h3>
            <p><strong>Element name:</strong> &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#lidoRecID">lido:lidoRecID</a>&gt;</p>
            <p><strong>Note:</strong> This mandatory element serves to
            distinguish an individual LIDO record from any other record
            that may occur in a database, data repository, or any other
            aggregation of machine-processable records. The LIDO
            Metadata Record Identifier is preferably composed of an
            identifier for the contributor and a record identification
            in the (local) system of the contributor. It is not required
            to be persistent. For more detailed information and examples
            see section <a
            href="#lido-metadata-record-identifier"><em>4.2.1 LIDO
            Metadata Record Identifier</em></a>.</p>
            <h3 data-number="5.1.3" id="objectwork-type"><span
            class="header-section-number">5.1.3</span> Object/Work
            Type</h3>
            <p><strong>Element name:</strong> &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#objectWorkType">lido:objectWorkType</a>&gt;</p>
            <p><strong>Note:</strong> The designation
            <strong>object/work</strong> dates from the CDWA Lite
            category <a
            href="https://www.getty.edu/research/publications/electronic_publications/cdwa/1object.html">Object/Work</a>,
            calling attention to the fact that the element embraces not
            only works of art, but, e.g., human-made everyday objects
            and natural specimens as well. This element captures the
            particular kind of an object, what the thing is in itself,
            considering its form and other intrinsic or defining
            characteristics. These generic properties are referred to as
            the <strong><em>isness</em></strong> of an object. Hence, a
            good descriptor for the object’s type conveys essential
            features of the object as required for reliable indexing and
            retrieval. The type of object at hand should always be
            indexed using the most specific descriptor available from
            the indexing vocabulary. Doing so is essential for ensuring
            a satisfactory level of precision in search results. In
            addition, there are more advantages when following the
            principle of the most specific and appropriate index entry
            (Cutter’s rule) if relying on a well-structured knowledge
            organization system. Specific descriptors for
            &lt;lido:objectWorkType&gt; may be found in the <a
            href="http://vocab.getty.edu/hier/aat/300264092">Objects
            Facet</a> hierarchy of the Art &amp; Architecture
            Thesaurus.</p>
            <p><strong>Example:</strong> In the following example the
            most specific concept from the Art &amp; Architecture
            Thesaurus (AAT), “<a
            href="http://vocab.getty.edu/page/aat/300033799">oil
            painting</a>”, is used to describe the work type of the
            <em>Mona Lisa</em> by Leonardo da Vinci. This statement is
            further qualified by the value for the type attribute, “<a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/lido00789">Object
            by material or technique</a>”, taken from the LIDO
            Terminology and corresponding to the equivalent “<a
            href="http://vocab.getty.edu/page/aat/300033701">guide
            term</a>” in the AAT hierarchy. The values for &lt;<a
            href="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel">skos:prefLabel</a>&gt;
            and &lt;<a
            href="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel">skos:altLabel</a>&gt;
            are supposed to be automatically taken from the AAT concept.
            In the case where the labels provided by the authority
            vocabulary are not well suited for display purposes, a
            &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#term">lido:term</a>&gt;
            with preference “<a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/lido00526">Display
            label</a>” may be added to control how the term will be
            presented. Terms for the <em>lido:type</em> attribute of
            Object/Work Type are provided by the LIDO <a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/objectWorkType_type">Object/Work
            Type Type-Vocabulary</a>.</p>
            <blockquote>
            <p><strong>Object/Work Type:</strong> Oil painting
            <strong><em>Preference:</em></strong> Display</p>
            </blockquote>
            <div class="sourceCode" id="cb21"><pre
            class="sourceCode xml"><code class="sourceCode xml"><span id="cb21-1"><a href="#cb21-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="co">&lt;!--Object by material or technique--&gt;</span></span>
<span id="cb21-2"><a href="#cb21-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>&lt;<span class="kw">lido:objectWorkType</span></span>
<span id="cb21-3"><a href="#cb21-3" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="ot">    lido:type=</span><span class="st">&quot;http://terminology.lido-schema.org/lido00789&quot;</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb21-4"><a href="#cb21-4" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>    &lt;<span class="kw">skos:Concept</span></span>
<span id="cb21-5"><a href="#cb21-5" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="ot">        rdf:about=</span><span class="st">&quot;http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300033799&quot;</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb21-6"><a href="#cb21-6" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>        &lt;<span class="kw">skos:prefLabel</span><span class="ot"> xml:lang=</span><span class="st">&quot;en&quot;</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb21-7"><a href="#cb21-7" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>            oil paintings (visual works)</span>
<span id="cb21-8"><a href="#cb21-8" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>        &lt;/<span class="kw">skos:prefLabel<